# Your library, deliberately outside version control.
#
# books.json holds every title you have read. Committing it publishes that list
# to anyone who can see the repo. Only the aggregates in data/public.json are
# committed, and they are regenerated automatically on every save.
data/books.json
data/books.backup.json

# Private notes live here, deliberately outside version control.
#
# public.json is committed and — on GitHub Pages — served publicly, so anything
# in it is published. Notes are private even from the local library file, so
# they are split into this companion file, which is never committed and never
# deployed. Losing it means losing the notes: back them up via the
# Goodreads CSV export, which does carry them.
data/notes.json
data/notes.backup.json

# Your Google Books API key and the lookup cache. The key is a credential and
# the cache is derived data, so neither belongs in the repo.
data/.google-books-key
data/genre-cache.json

# Written by tools/serve.py before each save.
data/public.backup.json
data/*.json.tmp

# Scratch CSVs. Exports from Goodreads/StoryGraph contain your full library and
# any private notes, so they stay out of the repo by default.
*.csv
!docs/examples/*.csv

.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
data/genre-run.log
data/launchd.log
